ent" dir="auto"><div class="TruncatedContent" tabindex="-1"><div class="TruncatedContent__text TruncatedContent__text--large" tabindex="-1" data-testid="contentContainer"><span class="Formatted">I had to literally force myself to finish this book. I feel like this did not have to be a “paranormal” genre because 90% was sex/lust. A black female lead being instantly fetishized and obsessed over in an uncomfortable way. If these two main characters were supposed to be ‘True Mates’ I would expect that their connection went BEYOND the constant sex and lust feelings. I felt like I was reading a fetish book!!! How about you go into detail about this so called unique, prophesied bond?<br /><br />The Vampire King just kept repeating the same sentiments over and over again, (1) he found his mate (2) all would respect their union or die (3) taming the so called monster within him. He was so CRINGE, dude what monster? Can we see it?? Cause throughout the book you were just all y’all no bite (expect during SEX, of course )<br /><br />This book could have been so much more interesting if we actually learned more about witches and her history. Only the prologue went into SOME kind of detail about witches. And then for the vampire and their coven, so much more could have been said and explored. The author just kept glazing over the actual paranormal things in this book to focus on sex.<br /><br />Btw how could 99% of of the book be this witch not knowing about her powers to at the last few pages... SUDDENLY she was so in control of all this power?? Smh mixed up priorities in this writing.<br /><br />I really didn’t like like this book, and it’s a shame because it could have been so amazing if it wasn’t a disguise for race play sex nonsense.</span></div><div class=""></div></div></section></section><section class="ReviewCard__tags"><ul class="CollapsableList"><span tabindex="-1"></span><span tabindex="-1"></span></ul></section><footer class="SocialFooter"><div data-testid="stats" cl